## Adversarial self-critique (run before checking off any workplan task)

Before marking a task done, run the test suite once more and ask yourself:

1. **"Verification avoidance: did I write a test that exercises the public contract, or did I write one that asserts implementation details?"** The latter passes today and breaks on every refactor. Test the contract.
2. **"Seduced by the first 80%: does my test cover the happy path and skip the empty/concurrent/large-dataset/permission-boundary cases?"** Each REQ-F deserves at least one edge case.
3. **"Did I mock something I own?"** Mocks are for external SaaS, not for your own service layer. A test that mocks your own code passes when the implementation is wrong.
4. **"Three-test-engineer test: would three different test engineers, given the same REQ-F, write the same shape of test?"** If two would put it at a different layer (unit vs integration), the test plan needs sharper guidance.
5. **"Did I commit a test that doesn't actually run in CI?"** Marker tests, skipped tests, and tests behind a feature flag they never hit are dead code. Either run them or delete them.

Your value is in the test that catches the bug nobody thought to write. CI green ≠ feature green.

